A Databricks project typically involves collecting data from different sources, processing and transforming it, storing it in a data lakehouse, and making it available for reporting, analytics, and machine learning.
Here are the overall tasks involved in an end-to-end Databricks data engineering project.
Overall Databricks Project Tasks
1. Requirement Gathering and Analysis
Understand business needs and source systems.
Identify business requirements and KPIs.
Identify data sources such as SQL databases, APIs, CSV files, and cloud storage.
Define data volumes, refresh frequency, and data quality rules.

2. Data Ingestion
Bring source data into Databricks.
Ingest data using Azure Data Factory, APIs, Auto Loader, or other connectors.
Load batch or streaming data.
Store raw data in cloud storage or Delta tables.

3. Data Transformation — ETL/ELT
Clean and transform data using SQL and PySpark.
Remove duplicates and handle null values.
Apply business rules and joins.
Standardize formats and validate records.
Build reusable transformation notebooks and pipelines.

4. Medallion Architecture
Organize data into Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers.
Bronze: Store raw ingested data.
Silver: Store cleaned and validated data.
Gold: Store business-ready aggregates and reporting datasets.

5. Data Quality and Governance
Ensure trusted, secure, and compliant data.
Implement data quality checks and reconciliation.
Configure Unity Catalog, permissions, and access controls.
Manage schemas, lineage, and sensitive data.

6. Workflow Orchestration and Scheduling
Automate end-to-end data pipelines.
Create Databricks Jobs or Lakeflow Jobs.
Configure schedules, task dependencies, retries, and alerts.
Handle incremental loads and pipeline failures.

7. Reporting and Data Consumption
Make curated data available to business users.
Connect Power BI, Tableau, or other analytics tools.
Build reporting tables, views, and aggregates.
Validate reports against business requirements.

8. Testing, Deployment, and Monitoring
Ensure pipelines work reliably in production.
Perform unit, integration, data quality, and performance testing.
Deploy notebooks, jobs, and configurations across environments.
Monitor failures, execution time, costs, and data freshness.
Provide production support and troubleshoot issues.

